The ordinary TRON Code uses planes 0x21 to 0x40 (although not all of these
are defined). "Extended TRON Code" defines some of the planes outside of
that range as well; the ordinary TRON planes are also used in Extended TRON
Code and no additions are made to them. (The Unicode-based planes are
deprecated in Extended TRON Code, but remain available for working with
data which is originally in Unicode format, and for cases where it is
necessary to use the characters as they are defined in Unicode.)

The arrangement of the TRON code is 0xVVPPXXXX where VV is the volume, PP
is the plane, and XXXX is the character code within the plane. The plane
and both bytes of the character code within the plane must be in the range
0x21 to 0xFD except 0x7F; the volume number can be anything (and it is a
part of the plane number, so the plane is within the volume).

Character codes within each plane and are split into four zones:
 A zone: High byte and low byte are both in range 0x21 to 0x7E.
 B zone: High byte in range 0x80 to 0xFD and low byte in range 0x21 to 0x7E.
 C zone: High byte in range 0x21 to 0x7E and low byte in range 0x80 to 0xFD.
 D zone: High byte and low byte are both in range 0x80 to 0xFD.

The encodings of TRON character set are:

* TRON-8: A character is represented by small-endian two-byte character
code, while a plane shift is one more byte 0xFE than the volume number,
followed by the one byte plane number; all further characters up to the
next plane shift will use the specified plane. Codes 0x00 to 0x20 and 0x7F
are reserved for control functions (which are not a part of TRON code).

* TRON-16: There is big-endian and small-endian variant. A plane shift is
0xFE21 to 0xFEFD for volume 0, or it can be any number of 0xFEFE followed
by either 0x0021 to 0x00FD (for odd volumes) or 0xFE21 to 0xFEFD (for even
volumes). A character code is 0x2121 to 0xFDFD. Control codes are in the
range 0x0000 to 0x0020 or 0x007F, and 0xFF21 to 0xFF7E are special codes.
The TRON Application Databus format also uses 0xFF80 to 0xFFFD.

* TRON-32: There is big-endian and small-endian variant. The codes are
simply the 32-bit code explained above, so the lowest normal character code
ix 0x00212121 and the highest is 0xFFFDFDFD. Other codes are not a part of
TRON code but may be used in other applications.

Some encodings in some planes use the "linear2hilo" function, which works
as follows:

* If 0 to 8836: Divide by 94. The high byte is 0x21 plus the quotient and
the low byte is 0x21 plus the remainder.

* If 8837 to 20679: Divide by 94. The high byte is 0x80 plus the quotient
and the low byte is 0x21 plus the remainder.

* If 20680 to 32523: Divide by 126. The high byte is 0x80 plus the quotient
and the low byte is 0x80 plus the remainder.

* If 32524 to 48399: Divide by 126. The high byte is 0x80 plus the quotient
and the low byte is 0x80 plus the remainder.

The other files in this directory are named as follows:

* The hexadecimal plane number (including volume number as well if it is
nonzero), if it is a file describing the characters (and other properties,
if applicable) of that plane. Some sets will span multiple planes; in this
case, the name will be hyphenated.

* X followed by other numbers for a draft specification. Sometimes an
extended plane may be partially defined, and the draft specification files
will be used for the parts that are not assigned yet. (For non-extended
planes, any drafts will be managed by separate Japanese organizations, and
are unlikely to be made available here or anywhere else.)
